WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.864
The plan is ready.

2
00:00:01.864 --> 00:00:04.193
The evidence ledger is organized.

3
00:00:04.193 --> 00:00:12.114
Yet the first person hearing the recommendation asks about delivery cost, while the second asks who benefits.

4
00:00:12.114 --> 00:00:18.170
Chapter Twenty-Three calls this the last-mile problem and introduces Persuasive Business Case GPT.

5
00:00:18.170 --> 00:00:22.830
That is a book case, not a live demonstration here.

6
00:00:22.830 --> 00:00:26.280
Return to the membership-service alternative from Lesson Thirteen.

7
00:00:26.280 --> 00:00:28.490
One decision owner manages operations.

8
00:00:28.490 --> 00:00:31.398
Another represents the people who use the service.

9
00:00:31.398 --> 00:00:36.864
They need different openings, but the underlying claim and source record must remain the same.

10
00:00:36.864 --> 00:00:40.470
Write the decision in one sentence before adjusting the pitch.

11
00:00:40.470 --> 00:00:45.529
Are we asking to run a bounded test, approve a permanent model, or fund a full rollout?

12
00:00:45.529 --> 00:00:48.088
A polished argument can blur those requests.

13
00:00:48.088 --> 00:00:50.123
State the smallest actual decision.

14
00:00:50.123 --> 00:00:51.926
Then check the evidence column.

15
00:00:51.926 --> 00:00:54.252
Which facts come from observed behavior?

16
00:00:54.252 --> 00:00:55.764
Which costs are estimates?

17
00:00:55.764 --> 00:00:57.858
Which reactions were only simulated?

18
00:00:57.858 --> 00:01:00.707
We can tailor the order in which we present them.

19
00:01:00.707 --> 00:01:05.301
We cannot promote an assumption into a fact because the room prefers certainty.

20
00:01:05.301 --> 00:01:10.186
The chapter's dashboard example shows how a business case changes with the audience.

21
00:01:10.186 --> 00:01:15.188
It is a story in the book; we will not repeat its outcome figures as current evidence.

22
00:01:15.188 --> 00:01:25.364
For our fictional test, the operations pitch begins with the workload: what staff would have to record, when they would review it, and which service promise remains unchanged.

23
00:01:25.364 --> 00:01:33.041
The member-facing pitch begins with the experience: how a person would understand the offer, enter it, and leave without a surprise.

24
00:01:33.041 --> 00:01:36.821
Both versions point to the same source note and the same unknown.

25
00:01:36.821 --> 00:01:39.205
Neither claims that conversion will rise.

26
00:01:39.205 --> 00:01:41.938
Put the common evidence strip under both cards.

27
00:01:41.938 --> 00:01:46.125
When you rewrite a sentence, compare it line by line against that strip.

28
00:01:46.125 --> 00:01:50.545
If the wording becomes stronger than the record, weaken it or seek evidence.

29
00:01:50.545 --> 00:01:52.348
This discipline protects trust.

30
00:01:52.348 --> 00:01:55.314
People will disagree about which risk matters most.

31
00:01:55.314 --> 00:01:59.734
They should not have to wonder whether they heard two versions of the truth.

32
00:01:59.734 --> 00:02:06.712
The question for the pitch is simple: what must this particular person decide, and what can the source actually support?

33
00:02:06.712 --> 00:02:10.469
A persuasive pitch must make the cost visible.

34
00:02:10.469 --> 00:02:18.452
The shorter-commitment test could require staff training, a clearer cancellation path, and a way to handle pauses.

35
00:02:18.452 --> 00:02:24.088
Those are possible operating requirements in our fictional exercise, not measured costs.

36
00:02:24.088 --> 00:02:27.845
Put them on the card before the recommendation.

37
00:02:27.845 --> 00:02:31.832
Add the option to keep the current model and the option to defer until the job is verified.

38
00:02:31.859 --> 00:02:32.614
Why test now?

39
00:02:32.614 --> 00:02:36.564
Because the evidence suggests a particular mismatch worth examining.

40
00:02:36.564 --> 00:02:37.667
Why a bounded test?

41
00:02:37.667 --> 00:02:41.617
Because the economics and user experience still have open questions.

42
00:02:41.617 --> 00:02:47.832
The book's pre-pitch workflow asks for objections before the meeting, when they can still improve the plan.

43
00:02:47.832 --> 00:02:53.060
It also identifies a common mistake: asking an assistant to make a weak case sound strong.

44
00:02:53.060 --> 00:02:53.931
Do the reverse.

45
00:02:53.931 --> 00:03:00.727
Ask which sentence needs a source, which cost is missing, and which stakeholder can block the test for a good reason.

46
00:03:00.727 --> 00:03:02.179
Then revise the proposal.

47
00:03:02.179 --> 00:03:06.245
If the answer is that the test cannot be run safely or fairly, say so.

48
00:03:06.245 --> 00:03:11.357
A decision brief is useful when it helps the group choose, including a decision to wait.

49
00:03:11.357 --> 00:03:15.132
A pre-pitch exercise can surface objections before the room does.

50
00:03:15.132 --> 00:03:18.269
Give the proposed test and its evidence to a reviewer.

51
00:03:18.269 --> 00:03:24.426
Ask them to challenge the weakest assumption, the delivery burden, and the risk of confusing participants.

52
00:03:24.426 --> 00:03:28.666
If an AI assistant produces the challenge, label it Simulation on screen.

53
00:03:28.666 --> 00:03:31.571
It has not spoken for operations staff or members.

54
00:03:31.571 --> 00:03:34.533
Turn each objection into a check with a real owner.

55
00:03:34.533 --> 00:03:36.218
Can the team support a pause?

56
00:03:36.218 --> 00:03:38.541
Ask the operator who manages enrollment.

57
00:03:38.541 --> 00:03:40.167
Does the offer read clearly?

58
00:03:40.167 --> 00:03:42.839
Test the wording with an approved participant.

59
00:03:42.839 --> 00:03:45.686
Would a funding partner need a different measure?

60
00:03:45.686 --> 00:03:48.358
Ask that partner what decision they must make.

61
00:03:48.358 --> 00:03:52.540
The book warns that persuasion does not replace the underlying judgment.

62
00:03:52.540 --> 00:03:54.108
Keep the response specific.

63
00:03:54.108 --> 00:03:56.664
“We have it handled” tells the room nothing.

64
00:03:56.664 --> 00:04:04.912
“We will review support tickets after the first cohort and stop if unresolved access questions rise” is a proposed plan that can be inspected.

65
00:04:04.912 --> 00:04:08.804
The precise threshold belongs in the learning plan before exposure.

66
00:04:08.804 --> 00:04:12.870
Rehearsal is valuable because it sharpens the next human conversation.

67
00:04:12.870 --> 00:04:17.066
Draft the first sixty-second version for the operations owner.

68
00:04:17.066 --> 00:04:24.060
Name the decision, the delivery change, the review date, and what would stop the test.

69
00:04:24.060 --> 00:04:28.257
Draft the second for the person accountable to members.

70
00:04:28.257 --> 00:04:34.318
Name the job, the proposed experience, the consent boundary, and what remains unverified.

71
00:04:34.318 --> 00:04:35.310
These are two ways into one decision.

72
00:04:35.334 --> 00:04:38.988
Annotate each claim with the source ID or mark it assumption.

73
00:04:38.988 --> 00:04:42.583
Where a source is absent, change the sentence to a question.

74
00:04:42.583 --> 00:04:44.081
Read both versions aloud.

75
00:04:44.081 --> 00:04:48.994
The first should sound like someone who has considered the work of implementation.

76
00:04:48.994 --> 00:04:54.086
The second should sound like someone who respects the burden of trying a new service.

77
00:04:54.086 --> 00:04:56.842
Neither should hide a condition in fine print.

78
00:04:56.842 --> 00:05:01.695
If the two versions lead listeners to different decisions, the pitch has drifted.

79
00:05:01.695 --> 00:05:04.331
Bring them back to the same bounded request.

80
00:05:04.331 --> 00:05:11.281
A source-linked brief lets someone disagree with your recommendation while still trusting your account of the facts.

81
00:05:11.281 --> 00:05:11.940
Pause here.

82
00:05:11.940 --> 00:05:14.756
Put both pitch versions into the decision file.

83
00:05:14.756 --> 00:05:21.047
Under each, list the claims, their exact source IDs, and one objection you still need a person to answer.

84
00:05:21.047 --> 00:05:26.739
Add the decision requested, the rejected alternative, the cost, the owner, and the review date.

85
00:05:26.739 --> 00:05:32.790
Resume when you can answer this question: what observation would make you change your recommendation?

86
00:05:32.790 --> 00:05:37.283
If you cannot answer, the argument is still a defense of a preferred story.

87
00:05:37.283 --> 00:05:45.371
Keep private deal terms, customer records, and internal conversations inside approved systems; this exercise uses a fictional proposal.

88
00:05:45.371 --> 00:05:47.828
Persuasive GPT remains a named book case.

89
00:05:47.828 --> 00:05:52.197
We have created an editorial teaching card, not proof of a deployed tool or a successful pitch.

90
00:05:52.197 --> 00:06:01.447
The next lesson asks what happens after one team learns from a test and other teams want to repeat it.

91
00:06:01.447 --> 00:06:01.489
Save the source strip.

92
00:06:01.497 --> 00:06:08.533
At scale, the same evidence will need to survive more meetings, more owners, and more chances for its meaning to drift.
